  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn upper-level low continues to linger over the Pacific Northwest\ntonight, providing ample Pacific moisture transport into the\nregion via southwest flow aloft. Winter weather impacts have\nalready been observed across Billings and Great Falls where heavy\nsnowfall has accumulated over highways. \n\nAcross the Glasgow CWA, rainfall is still being observed and \ntiming on a rain-to-snow transition is uncertain, especially\nacross lower elevations. Temperatures have trended slightly \ncooler than originally forecasted, so it is possible for \nprecipitation to start mixing earlier than previous forecast \nguidance. Meanwhile, anticipated snowfall totals may be \nheightened especially in high elevations of the Little Rockies and\nalong the Canadian border. Area cameras snow accumulating snow on\nHighway 191 south of Malta near Zortman this evening. Confidence \nremains below average on forecast accumulations due to a wide \nrange expected due to differences in elevation as well as marginal\nthermal profiles.\n\nForecast QPF totals have continued to trend upward, and much of\nthe region can anticipate half an inch to an inch of additional\nrainfall. Wibaux and southeastern Prairie Counties should remain \non the drier side with rainfall amounts up to 0.50\" possible. Snow\naccumulation will remain more concentrated in areas described\nabove, while lower elevations may struggle to accumulate much \nsnow due to warm ground temperatures. Winter Weather Advisories \nremain in effect until noon Monday for Southwest Phillips, \nNorthern Phillips, and Northern Valley Counties as a result.\n\nTemperatures remain well below average tomorrow before gradually\nwarming up by the end of the week. An upper-level cutoff low will\ndevelop over the Four Corners region and advect warmer air into\nNortheast Montana. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n\nConfidence of below average temperatures through Monday remain\nhigh. There is moderate confidence in a gradual temperature climb\nthroughout the week, but uncertain on exact temperature profiles. \nChances of additional precipitation later in the week will depend\non the track of the system over the Four Corners.\n\nWhile confidence in QPF amounts have trended higher, precipitation\ntype, timing, and snow accumulation remain below average. \nEvaporative cooling effects have likely contributed to cooler-\nthan-forecasted temperatures, but warm ground temperatures still\nremain and will prevent much accumulation in lower elevations. \n\nModel agreement deteriorates drastically beyond Wednesday, so \nconfidence remains low for weather conditions mid- week onward.\n\nKENZ",
